{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S81-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"dab23208149e775d69b3989c4883ef1a331cb7f49211ccc2962121ec4926cafc\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers practical rescue and A5 returns to fixing, but neither establishes this as A's usual relationship role.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's listening request; A5 redirects fixing toward tea after B4 excludes fixing people.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants listening and B4 protects that wish, but A1 leaves their shared history unspecified.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 preserves the fixing impulse through tea, though it reduces rather than substantially extends A1's intervention.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies upset, tea and a possible telephone call; B2 gives an immediately playable emotional need.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's tea-temperature workaround earns its joke from B4's refusal of repair.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You're crying, love. I'll make tea and ring your manager, like last time.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Mum, I've been promoted. Could you just listen?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. Will they give you enough staff?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Yes. Just raise your cup and say congratulations.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Congratulations. Now, who covers you when you're ill?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S81 makes A immediately resume an established relationship role despite a present need for different behavior.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General compulsive helpfulness can look similar without any evidence that fixing is this relationship's familiar division of labor.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"The original A1 and A5 show fixing but omit its history; revised A1's 'like last time' and B2's 'Mum' establish the returning role.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses parental protection during good news; alternative b uses a habitual accomplice trying to remain useful during an honest apology.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original lacked an established role. The revision introduces a mother and newly promoted child. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. A3 converts B2's promotion into staffing concern; B4's toast briefly succeeds before A5 resumes maternal contingency planning. Five spoken turns and under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 makes a modest escalation into continuing workplace oversight; its effectiveness depends on sincere concern rather than a knowingly comic mother.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S81-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"921dbe05993bdb5bc400e8a8082ac3245110f36256883a887f984b67a3b89f6f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's 'usual excuse' establishes habitual covering, and A3 converts honesty into another editing assignment.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's proposed apology, but A5 asks B4 to assign a role rather than demonstrably returning to it.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants honest accountability and B4 wants independence from A's established assistance.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 retreats to asking for a part after B4 instead of extending A1's familiar covering behavior.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies recurring lateness and a prepared excuse, though the apology's recipient remains unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'tidy the wording' neatly exposes how A absorbs B2's honesty into the old arrangement.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You're late for Mum's birthday again. I've told her your train broke down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm telling her I overslept. You always cover for me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Right. I'll explain how exhausted you've been.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Just come in with me. Don't explain anything.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'll look exhausted too. She'll see it runs in the family.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S81 makes A resume the customary role in this particular relationship when the present situation calls for stepping out of it.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary dishonesty would explain an excuse but would not explain A repeatedly reclaiming the sibling's established protective role.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 names the usual excuse; revised B2 explicitly names habitual covering, and revised A5 finds a silent way to continue it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b turns a sibling's request for accompaniment into nonverbal cover; alternative a turns a requested celebration into maternal risk management.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the original A5's role-seeking with an actual return to covering. New setting: siblings entering their mother's birthday celebration. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 5, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 95. B2's oversleeping produces A3's exhaustion defense; B4's silence constraint produces A5's proposed appearance. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 describes a proposed performance within spoken dialogue; actors should preserve A's sincere belief that this will help.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S82-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"064b006f719d992164ae568d2ab9188f395655de2f8e7406bf2981b4886906b9\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 compares frame sizes; A3 interprets B2's photograph size as greater space for Sam; A5 compares the meaning of equal treatment.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 specifically reinterprets B2's bigger photograph, and A5 makes B4's matching-frame solution conditional.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a practical explanation and B4 offers repair, changing A's complaint from inequality to compensatory equality.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 demands that B4's equality also appear unprompted, making reassurance harder to supply.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's two displayed photographs and unequal frames make the comparison immediately visible and playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'more Sam' compresses the emotional misreading, and A5's compensation condition follows directly from B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 94\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Sam's photo has a bigger frame than mine.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It was a bigger photograph.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So you had room for more Sam.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can buy matching frames.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Only if mine doesn't look like compensation.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S82 converts attention or affection into a comparison with what a competing connection appears to receive.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General insecurity seeks reassurance without necessarily measuring one's treatment against another person's.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 names Sam's larger frame, A3 reads its size as emotional allocation, and A5 keeps comparing even after B4 offers equality.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a bargains over equal treatment at home; alternative b disputes the distribution of ceremonial affection through invitations.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged after a second internal reread with the same ratings and total of 94. B4's helpful matching-frame offer causes A5 to demand equality without the stigma of compensation. All spoken-turn, causal and length gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The exact family or romantic relationship remains open, and A5's concern about appearances benefits from a sincere rather than arch delivery.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S82-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"d26c26fc08ca112ca2f6c798afcd37e0be6acffde46654af520f5fad01093d85\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 compares ribbon with staple and A3 makes it an affection queue, but A5 weakens the explicit comparison with another connection.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's shortage, but B4's 'first invitation' leaves the timing of that shortage unresolved.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 supply explanations, but B4 mostly introduces a reversal for A5 to answer.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 rejects priority as reassurance, though 'stationery instead of love' is less specific than A3's queue.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's ribbon and staple make the unequal invitations concrete; B4 complicates their production history.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's affection queue is compact and grounded in B2, while A5 becomes a broader aphoristic complaint.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Clare's wedding invitation has a ribbon. Mine has a staple.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're my sister. I gave you the first one, before the ribbon arrived.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So Clare got the version you were proud of.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Take my ribbon. I'm the bride.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Will Clare know? Otherwise she'll still think you like her more.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S82 makes A assess each sign of affection by comparing it with what another connection receives.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A complaint about shoddy presentation could concern an invitation's quality without treating that quality as a ranking of relationships.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A3 explicitly calls the distribution an affection queue; revised A3 and A5 compare the bride's pride and apparent liking for Clare.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b adds a sibling's concern about the rival's knowledge and the bride's personal sacrifice; alternative a worries that matching frames will look compensatory.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarified the ribbon chronology and restored a concrete comparative A5. New setting: a bride and her sister inspecting invitations. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's early delivery becomes A3's inferior draft; B4's personal ribbon offer becomes A5's demand for visible comparative status. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 moves into public comparison rather than private reassurance; it should remain a vulnerable request rather than calculated social domination.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S83-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"caa1919021a29ba915031b8b970cf78d31d65d58b7545d65d40e4fbdb1a85047\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks about the key before remembering B has not visited; A3 reflexively redirects another uncertainty to the same person.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's exclusion from the flat choice prompts A3's smaller question; B4's left-pocket answer produces A5's discovery.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 registers hurt over exclusion, yet B4 still supplies intimate practical knowledge.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns successful help into a claim about their remaining partnership, but adds little new uncertainty or action.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's new flat and B4's habitual pocket make changed circumstances and retained familiarity concrete.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's retreat from choosing a flat to finding a key is economical; A5 lightly explains its emotional implication.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Which key opens my new flat? Sorry, you haven't been there.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You chose it without me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Right. Which pocket would I have put the key in?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your left, usually.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"There it is. We still work on the smaller decisions.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S83 makes uncertainty trigger consultation of this person before A notices that the old habit has operated.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S89 seeks evidence of continuing specialness deliberately, whereas S83 begins with an unconsidered practical appeal.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1's question precedes its correction, and A3 repeats the practical consultation after B2 exposes how their circumstances have changed.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a lets a hurt former decision partner help with a smaller task; alternative b uses a coat consultation and departure logistics to repeat the reflex.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained unchanged after a second internal reread with the same ratings and total of 89. B4's correct pocket answer earns A5's small claim of continuing cooperation. The restrained development passes without needing an extra punchline.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 approaches relationship interpretation more than renewed reflex; the unusually clear A1 and A3 carry the exact card.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S83-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b4095982c561427bb9290e566e845d386db6a8b783685947b49a028b5a749eb1\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 notices an unnecessary call after making it, but A3 and A5 develop telephone absurdity more than uncertainty-driven consultation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains B2's objection, but A5's report of 'how it went' barely uses B4's blue-coat answer.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"B2 corrects the redundant call and B4 supplies a coat color, with little independent stake or changed choice.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 proposes another redundant communication without introducing an uncertainty that triggers the card.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a coat choice and physical proximity, but A5 leaves the event being reported unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's worried thumb is concise, but A5's hang-up joke detaches from B4's practical help.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 50\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Which coat should I wear to the interview? Sorry, I still ring you before opening the wardrobe.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The blue one. You left it here when you moved out.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Can I collect it at eight? Sorry. What's convenient for you?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Eight's fine. I'll leave it outside; I'm going away.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Will I need an umbrella? Sorry, you're not coming with me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S83 makes A involve a familiar person at the first moment of uncertainty and recognize the habit only afterward.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Being dependent or indecisive alone does not supply the required sequence of automatic consultation followed by recognition.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A1 supplies that sequence once; revised A1, A3 and A5 each place the appeal before the correction.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b repeats the reflex during an interview departure despite B's absence; alternative a discovers that a former decision partner still knows a useful small detail.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Replaced the disconnected telephone ending with linked coat and departure uncertainties, preserving the intimate practical-choice seed. New setting: an interview morning after moving out. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 4, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 88. B2's retained coat causes A3's collection question; B4's outdoor pickup and absence cause A5's weather consultation. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's umbrella uncertainty is a modest extension of outdoor collection; the repeated apologies must sound newly noticed, not rehearsed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S84-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7953834f94c7697e713277ce3b131979c9931c9afe433a2b088c48ec9c7d1667\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A1 already maintains a birthday grievance; B2 and B4 expose calendar markings rather than provide small new disappointments that reopen hurt.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains B2's circle and A5 accounts for B4's next-year discovery.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 investigate the alleged forgiveness, but have little opportunity to disappoint or repair in the present.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 expands the calendar surveillance into next year, and A5 endorses it without reopening a newly specified wound.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's missed birthday and B4's next-year circle are concrete and easy to stage through dialogue.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's administrative closure and A5's advance notice sustain a concise bureaucratic treatment of forgiveness.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 62\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm nearly over you missing my birthday. Shall we book dinner?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Thursday, unless work needs me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's what you said before I ate both birthday puddings.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Friday, then. I'll put a reminder in my phone.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Thank you. Make it say my name. Last year's just said 'dinner'.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S84 lets a small present disappointment reopen one precise piece of hurt while A is behaving as though reconciliation is nearly complete.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Holding a grudge continuously lacks the card's movement from apparent recovery back into a specific wound.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original B2 and B4 merely uncover monitoring; revised B2's work caveat recalls the abandoned puddings, and B4's reminder recalls an impersonal entry.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a accepts a repair but finds another painful detail inside it; alternative b reopens the hurt through careless joking and an inadequate apology.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The original illustrated surveillance of an old grievance. The revision keeps birthday hurt but introduces a reconciliation dinner. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's conditional booking causes A3's exact memory; B4's helpful reminder causes A5 to expose another precise surviving hurt. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's disappointment is in the reminder's resemblance to the previous failure, not a fresh broken promise; the grateful opening helps preserve near-forgiveness.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S84-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"0ec83ef0886f66b578f507d2dcf95aa8719d8df6cf33da3ad7f8eefd24cf0511\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims resolution and A3 specifies hurt at being mocked, but B2 only identifies a joke that the dialogue never actually supplies.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 clarifies B2's question, and A5 answers B4's claim that they had moved on.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants the matter closed, but B2's clarification gives limited evidence of the disappointing behavior A describes.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 restates renewed grievance through a vase metaphor rather than revealing a further precise part of the hurt.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a broken vase but refers to 'that particular joke' without letting actors hear its content.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'brought the vase with us' follows B4's moving-on language economically.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 66\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Put your flowers here. I'm nearly over the vase, honestly.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"In a mug? Harder for me to improve the decor.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You called it ugly while I was picking up Mum's signature.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Sorry. I forgot she'd painted it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's the bit. You helped her choose the blue. I thought you'd remember.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S84 makes attempted reconciliation vulnerable to small disappointments that reopen exact details of a past injury.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A general accusation of insensitivity need not show an initially recovering relationship or separate present triggers for remembered hurt.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 welcomes flowers, B2's decor joke reopens the insult in A3, and B4's forgetting reopens the shared history in A5.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b uses a present joke followed by forgetfulness; alternative a uses scheduling and a helpful reminder, allowing repair itself to renew the hurt.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Made the missing joke audible and gave the apology a consequential omission. New setting: flowers offered after breaking a hand-painted family vase. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's forgotten authorship directly causes A5's more intimate memory of choosing paint together. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The handmade family object gives this alternative a more tender, less lightly comic register than the birthday scene.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S85-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ee684000fc2e6a269359349c3697510850d7bd5b02a2a034e118f253fbf99d3e\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 notices easy hugging and offers biscuits, but it is unclear whether A envies B's appeal or others' access to B's affection.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 twists B2's 'didn't have to' toward effortless hugs; A5 accepts B4's invitation while revealing preparation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers inclusion, but B2 and B4 reveal little about which relationship or competition matters to A.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's rehearsed spontaneity extends performance after B4, though it leaves the competitive aim diffuse.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies favorite biscuits and observed hugs; B4 locates an inviting group.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's rehearsed spontaneous smile is concise and follows B4's invitation, despite being somewhat self-aware.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You arrive at family lunch empty-handed and everyone hugs you. It's lovely. I've baked three cakes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"They'd hug you without cake. Come sit beside me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you introduce me before mentioning the cakes? Give me a fair start.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You're my brother. They know you. Leave the cakes here.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"All three? Fine. Save me whichever seat gets hugged first.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S85 combines admiration and resentment of another person's effortless affection with A's attempt to become equally worth choosing.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"S82 compares shares of affection; S85 additionally makes A compete by presenting themself as equally lovable.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Original A5 supplies effortful presentation but leaves its rival unclear; revised A1 admires B's reception and A3 requests a fair personal audition.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a competes for family warmth through gifts and seating; alternative b rehearses a greeting to compete with a housemate's effortless welcome.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Clarified who is effortlessly loved and what A is competing for. New setting: siblings at family lunch. Separate internal reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's claim that cake is unnecessary causes A3's attempted fresh introduction; B4 removes the cakes, so A5 seeks the best opportunity for affection directly. Five spoken turns, under 75 words.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5's hugged-first seat is an anxious attempt to optimize a welcome; an overly knowing delivery could turn it into a detached joke.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S85-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"bf6482477c09829511bd963b04193c495d4fb18fa6742bf99394b4def264b652\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 admires B's greeting and A3 seeks lovability, but the unfairness and competitive wish to be chosen remain faint.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 attempts B2's normal hello, and A5 responds directly to B4's volume criticism.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 function mainly as performance feedback without a clear personal want or social stake.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 merely lowers the volume after B4 rather than increasing commitment or consequence in competing for affection.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes greeting practice but no occasion, relationship or particular audience.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'effortlessly lovable' and B4's 'loud' create a compact, causally connected contrast.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 63\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Our new housemates adore your sleepy hello. It's wonderful. Unfair, but wonderful. Listen to mine.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm making breakfast. Just say hello.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Hello. Lovely people. Would you choose to live with that?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We already chose you. Pass the toast.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Of course. Did you hear how naturally I said that? You could wake up to it daily.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S85 makes admiration of another's easy affection feel unfair and turns that feeling into a competing display of personal appeal.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Social-skills rehearsal can imitate an admired greeting without resentment or a wish to rival the other person's desirability.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 explicitly joins admiration with unfairness, while A3 and A5 pitch A as someone the housemates should keep choosing.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b redirects a housemate's ordinary breakfast request into another audition; alternative a abandons gift-based effort but still competes for the warmest reception.\